[Music]  in our school matters series we're  looking at the effort to keep students  from dropping out because of the  pandemic nationwide  it's estimated about 3 million students  are unaccounted for  and the experts fear that that could  have long-term consequences for their  education  we visited sunset high school that's in  dallas which is an area that is hard hit  by this pandemic  educators in the districts are trying to  track down more than 9  000 high school students nine thousand  who stopped attending classes there  maria villarreal shows us one student's  struggle  to just stay on track i love going to  school  just for the interactions and getting  along with everyone else but  something about teaching was just so  different and something very unique  that i was like i might as well give it  a shot so you want to be a teacher  yes before the pandemic luis borja  enrolled in sunset high school  to take advantage of their college  credit program but in the midst of covid  before his senior year  luis decided to start working did you  want to help out your family  i wanted to you know they said you don't  have to do it but i was like  you know there's bills that need to be  paid but i also want to learn how to  take  responsibility you know and be  independent  but balancing work in online summer  classes caused luis to fall behind with  his schoolwork  and he was placed on academic dismissal  i thought my world came  crashing down that day because it was  four years you know work we just  continued to encounter different types  of problems that we  really weren't prepared for principal  claudia vega has been working with  students  including luis to get them back on track  and back in school  you know we're missing anywhere from 15  to 20 percent  and the failure rate is 15 to 20 percent  and  we've got to find a way to bring them  back we've got to enrollment sits at  2100 students  but today even though the school has  been fully open  for in-person learning since the fall  just 279  kids have shown up we tied along with  dallas isd administrators  as a search for high school students  that had disappeared during the pandemic  more than nine thousand total including  more than fifteen hundred seniors the  reason we're going and making these  visits is to let them know  that we understand and that we have  programs in place to help them  across the country it's estimated that  schools have lost contact  with more than three million students  during the pandemic  unfortunately none of the five houses we  were not able to make any contact we're  going to get these kids back in school  at some point we're getting them back in  school that same grit  is what created a new norm at sunset  high school  i think that we've learned a lot in this  past year and a half  we've learned to be flexible we've  learned that kids  like to have more choices one of those  choices includes online learning during  the day  but also offering a homework hub from  five to seven at night  that's staffed with rotating teachers  like our education system hasn't changed  in  generations right like decades and so  our teachers had always kind of said  let's do something different  let's try and then we were forced into  this opportunity where  you just had to how do you call it an  opportunity  um oh my goodness  what is it that makes makes you so  emotional about this we've had  oh give me one second of course we've  had  employees hospitalized we've had  students  hospitalized we've our students  um have suffered they've struggled  they've lost parents they've lost  siblings um we had a family that  lost four members in their immediate  family  and students in 10th grade and we're  trying to convince them to stay in  school  how do you do that every way possible  and principal vega's persistence paid  off  luis is now back on track set to  graduate with a high school  and associates degree this may would you  say maybe the pandemic  forced you to become a better student  yes  it made me become a student that i never  realized i could be  you know i knew i always could be  independent  but i didn't know what motivation i  needed what needed to light up me up  to do it you know overall yes it shaped  me up into a  whole different person but i'd say  not a negative way in a better way for  cbs this morning  mideagle dallas oh  boy i hope other kids make the same  choice to go back yeah stakes are so  high  it's been such a tough year for  everybody everybody for the kids for the  teachers yeah

